INSERT INTO `Document` VALUES ('0883e563-81e6-43d0-a1c9-6593261df939','6c58df0c-3655-49fe-92a9-b309e89d4316','ICISS_2026_paper_126 (1).pdf','ICISS_2026_paper_126 (1).pdf','PENDING','Hierarchical Multi-Layer Reranking Approach for\nBiomedical Retrieval-Augmented Generation\nAbstract—Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) enhances\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) for domain-specific tasks such\nas medical question answering. However, recent reviews of RAG\napplications in the medical domain indicate that issues such\nas retrieval noise remain and require further investigation into\nmitigation strategies, with several prior works attempting to ad-\ndress this challenge. This study explores a multi-layer reranking\napproach that combines common RAG retrieval with topic and\nsemantic tag similarity as ”booster”, rewarding documents with\nmore relevant topics and tags. The main focus is on analyzing\nthe contribution of multiple retrieval signals within a hierarchical\nreranking framework by using structured biomedical information\nsuch as PubMed—including MeSH-based topics—to refine doc-\nument ranking. An ablation analysis comparing no reranking,\nfull reranking, tag- and topic-only reranking was conducted\nat the retrieval level. Retrieval results across Recall@K shows\nhigh results, which means it is able to return relevant medical\ndocuments with the reranking method compared to the baseline\nconfiguration, with Recall@K reaching around 98.38% while\nbaseline resulted in 97.92%. However, MRR result shows a slight\ndecrease, indicating trade-offs in top-rank document ordering.\nGeneration quality, evaluated using BLEU-4 and ROUGE, is\ncomparatively lower. In contrast, domain-specific BERTScore\n(with a biomedical encoder) indicates high semantic similarity\nbetween generated and truth answers, averaging 96.99%. Over-\nall, the results indicate that incorporating topic similarity and\nsemantic tag signals can improve structured retrieval in domain-\nspecific RAG systems.\nIndex Terms—RAG, Large Language Models, Medical Ques-\ntion Answering, Biomedical Information Retrieval, Hierarchical\nReranking.\nI. INTRODUCTION\nThis research examines a comprehensive Retrieval-\nAugmented Generation (RAG) pipeline within the medical\nfield. We emphasize the implementation of topic- and tag-\nbased retrieval mechanisms. As biomedical knowledge grows\nquickly in both quantity and complexity [1], large language\nmodels and RAG-based systems are becoming more and more\nimportant for finding useful information [2]. Previous studies\nhave shown that structured representations can help enhance\nretrieval relevance and performance [3]. However, there is still\na lot of work to be done in this area. How can document\nstructure and metadata representations contribute to improving\nretrieval relevance in a RAG pipeline, and can topic- and\ntag-based retrieval approaches be effectively implemented in\nthe medical domain? This research aims to evaluate whether\nadditional topic and tag signals can improve retrieval relevance\nand answer quality in a medical Retrieval-Augmented Gener-\nation (RAG) system. We examine the feasibility of evaluating\nthe similarity between a query and the retrieved segments,\nas well as with the topics and semantic tags, and utilize\nthese indicators as score boosters in the final retrieval scoring.\nThe study also draws on recent cutting-edge methods [4] in\nmulti-layer reranking for medical question answering, using\nlayered retrieval refinement in the pipeline design. Then, their\nstandard metrics are used to measure retrieval and generation\nperformance under the specified settings. The scope is limited\nto biomedical literature, specifically of PubMed abstracts, em-\nploying a singular extensive language model—Mistral LLM,\ncreated by Mistral AI—alongside specialized embedding mod-\nels and a specific vector database configuration. This work\noffers organized insights into the use of topic and tag-based\nrepresentations, as well as layered reranking strategies, for\nretrieval in domain-specific medical RAG systems. In the\nmedical domain, recent advancements in LLMs and RAG\nfurther motivate this work. Large Language Models (LLMs)\nhave rapidly progressed and are increasingly used in health-\ncare for tasks such as clinical decision support, question\nanswering, and information retrieval, but they face limitations\nin domain-specific accuracy and may generate hallucinated\noutputs, posing risks in critical settings [5], [6]. To tackle\nthese challenges, Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) has\nemerged as a promising approach for improving information\nretrieval. By combining large language models with selected\nknowledge, RAG reduces hallucination and grounds answers\nto medical literature [6], [7]. For example, RAGMed [8], and\napplications in subdomain-specific tasks like dementia care\n[9] demonstrates RAG efficacy in evidence-based question\nanswering, guideline-supported decision systems, and biomed-\nical information retrieval.\nII. LITERATURE REVIEW\nA. Large Language Models\nLarge Language Models (LLMs) are trained on large text\ndataset to learn grammar, language patterns, and contextual\npatterns, enabling them to perform tasks such as question\nanswering, summarization, and text generation [10]. In special-\nized domains such as medicine, LLMs use their learned lan-\nguage patterns and knowledge to perform tasks such as entity\nrecognition, pattern recognition, inference, classification, and\nquestion answering [11], but they may struggle with domain-\nspecific knowledge and can produce hallucinations, which is\ncritical in the medical domain. While increasing model size\nimproves performance across various NLP tasks without task-\nspecific fine-tuning [12], this also increases computational and\nfinancial costs [13] and still has hallucination issues [14].\nAs a result, retrieval-based methods have been developed to\ntackle these limitations without requiring resource-intensive\n\n-- 1 of 6 --\n\ntraining [15]. This encourages approaches such as Retrieval-\nAugmented Generation (RAG) to improve accuracy in domain-\nspecific tasks.\nB. Retrieval-Augmented Generation\nRetrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) improves Large\nLanguage Models by retrieving external information rather\nthan relying on learned internal knowledge, providing it with\nrelevant data from external sources to produce responses\nthat are more specific and supported by evidence [16]. This\nstudy extends the standard pipeline by incorporating additional\nstructured retrieval signals—topic similarity and semantic tag\nmatching—within a multi-stage reranking process, where these\nsignals are included in the scoring mechanism to improve\nretrieval relevance by prioritizing documents aligned with both\nthe query and relevant metadata. The pipeline applies recursive\nchunking [17] and vector retrieval, and then Mistral-based\nLLM makes the response. Evaluation is mostly about retrieval\nperformance, and response quality is seen as a downstream\noutcome.\nC. Knowledge Ingestion and Retrieval Methods\nKnowledge ingestion is the process of gathering, transform-\ning, and organizing domain-specific information for efficient\nuse in a RAG system [18]. Documents are segmented into\nsmaller “chunks” using methods such as semantic or recursive\nchunking to maintain manageable context sizes, then embed-\nded into a vector via embedding models and stored in a vector\ndatabase for fast access. Retrieval is triggered when a user\nsubmits a query, which is embedded using the same model, and\nsimilarity scores are computed against stored document vectors\nto select the top-k chunks for the generation pipeline [19]. This\nstudy integrates structured retrieval mechanisms—topic-based\nsimilarity and semantic tag similarity—to better align queries\nand documents, incorporating these signals into a multi-layer\nreranking process that refines retrieved results and improves\noverall relevance.\nD. Generation and Evaluation Methods\nDuring the generation stage, the query and retrieved doc-\nument chunks are combined and provided to the LLM as a\nsingle input, allowing the model to generate answers using\nboth its internal knowledge and the supplied context, which\nhelps reduce hallucinations in domain-specific scenarios; in\nmulti-turn settings, previous interactions can also be included\nto maintain context [19]. A Mistral-based LLM agent handles\ngeneration in this work as part of the generation pipeline. Eval-\nuation takes into account both how well the retrieval works and\nhow good the outputs are. Common metrics like BLEU and\nROUGE use surface-level text overlap, which doesn’t always\nshow how accurate the information is, especially when an-\nswering open-ended medical questions [20], while BERTScore\n[21] measures similarity between generated answer and truth\nanswer. Because of this, the analysis focuses more on retrieval\nperformance, which is measured by Recall@K, Precision@K,\nand Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR), and also BERTScore.\nE. Related Work\nRecent studies have shown the effectiveness of Retrieval-\nAugmented Generation (RAG) in medical applications, in-\ncluding clinical text generation, decision support systems,\nand medical question answering [22] [23]. However, a re-\ncent review of RAG applications in the medical domain [7]\nhighlights that, while RAG systems are capable of retrieving\npertinent information, challenges such as retrieval noise still\npersist, requiring further exploration of mitigation strategies.\nSeveral prior works have addressed this issue. Multi-stage\nRAG frameworks such as Bailicai [24] improved domain adap-\ntation and reduced hallucination through enhanced retrieval\nand knowledge, while at the same time, endured noise-related\nchallenges that come with traditional RAG. Frameworks such\nas CLI-RAG [25] introduce hierarchical chunking and multi-\nstage retrieval to better handle structured clinical data. Another\nrecent study such as AT-RAG [26], uses topic-based mecha-\nnisms to improve retrieval efficiency and relevance by filtering\ndocuments before retrieval. These shows that structured repre-\nsentations, such as topics, can enhance retrieval performance,\nparticularly in complex or multi-hop question answering tasks.\nF. Gap Analysis\nAlthough prior work has improved retrieval quality through\nmulti-stage pipelines and structured representations, retrieval\nnoise remains an ongoing issue in medical RAG implemen-\ntation. Existing methods primarily rely on single structured\nsignals or filtering, such as topics, and do not fully explore the\ncombined use of multiple signals for reranking. In particular,\nthe integration of multiple structured signals — such as\ntopic similarity and semantic tags — as a unified reranking\nlayer remains underexplored. This limits the ability to fully\ncapture document relevance to specific medical queries. This\nstudy presents a hierarchical multi-layer reranking method\nthat uses topic similarity and semantic tag signals to improve\nretrieval relevance and reduce noise. It is evaluated on medical\nquestion-answering tasks using PubMedQA to examine how\nthese signals affect retrieval performance. This study presents\ninsights into the individual and combined impact of topic and\ntag signals in domain-specific RAG pipelines.\nIII. METHODOLOGY\nThis study uses an experimental pipeline to explore a\nhierarchical approach to multi-reranking in RAG within the\nmedical field. It implements a complete pipeline with inges-\ntion, retrieval, and generation stages. The evaluation centers\non retrieval effectiveness, especially the impact of structured\nsimilarity signals like topic and semantic tag representations in\na multi-layer reranking process. Retrieval quality is measured\nwith standard metrics, and generation quality is assessed as\na result of retrieval. The setup will be defined to ensure\nconsistency in evaluation, allowing clear insight into how\nthese setup and the structured retrieval affects both information\nrelevance and response quality.\n\n-- 2 of 6 --\n\nA. Dataset Description\nThis study exclusively uses biomedical literature for inges-\ntion. PubMed abstracts of medical research form the knowl-\nedge corpus. A PubMed search query is compiled for each\nevaluation query to retrieve relevant documents. The RAG\npipeline uses 100 abstracts per query as its knowledge base,\nresulting in 21,537 data points.\nB. PubMed Data Mining Pipeline\nA query-specific knowledge corpus is compiled during data\nmining for retrieval-augmented generation. A structured search\nquery is created for each evaluation question using MeSH list\nprovided within the PubMedQA dataset [27]. This ensures\nthat the retrieved documents match the evaluation query. A\nfixed amount of top results (100 articles per query) is selected\nto enrich the knowledge base. These fetched article IDs are\niteratively passed to the PubMed NCBI EFetch API, which\nretrieves the document XML along with the metadata. The\nprocess is shown in Figure 1.\nFig. 1. Mining pipeline for PubMed article.\nC. RAG Pipeline Architecture\nOur pipeline will add topic similarity check and tag sim-\nilarity check on all retrieved chunks in the retrieval stage.\nThis pipeline is designed to process biomedical literature\nand generate responses to medical questions, consisting of\nthree main stages: ingestion, retrieval, and generation. The\narchitecture diagram is shown in Figure 2.\nThe pipeline operates through the following stages:\n1) Knowledge Ingestion During ingestion, parsed abstracts\nare divided into smaller chunks using a recursive chunk-\ning method with predefined chunk size and overlap\nparameters. Each chunk is then converted into vector\nembeddings using the mistral-embed model. These em-\nbeddings are stored in a vector database to support\nsemantic similarity retrieval.\n2) Retrieval In this stage, the input query is embedded\ninto a vector representation using a dedicated embedding\nmodel. An initial set of candidate chunks is retrieved\nthrough vector similarity search. These retrieved candi-\ndates then undergo additional cosine similarity compu-\ntations based on the associated topic and semantic tags\nattached to each chunk candidate.\nThe retrieved candidates are further refined through a\nhierarchical multi-layer reranking process incorporating\nadditional similarity signals, including topic-based simi-\nlarity and semantic tag similarity. Similarity scoring for\nboth layers is performed using a cross-encoder variant\nof MedCPT [28], a biomedical domain-specific cross-\nencoding model trained to capture query-chunk semantic\nsimilarity.\nUsing chunk similarity, topic similarity, and tag simi-\nlarity scores, the final ranking score is computed as a\nweighted combination of these signals:\nFinalScore = (0.75 × Schunk) + (0.20 × Stag)\n+ (0.05 × Stopic) (1)\nThese weights represent the hierarchical importance of\neach retrieval layer, where the primary vector similarity\nscore contributes the largest portion of the final ranking.\nTopic (Stopic) and tag (Stag) similarities are treated as\na final score booster, providing additional points to the\nbase score. This ensures query–document similarity to\nremain as the main score factor, rewarding documents\nthat have high topic and tag similarity to the query.\nD. Retrieval Setup And Configuration\nIn the retrieval flow, 20 candidates are retrieved via vector\nsearch, reranked through the multilayer pipeline, and narrowed\ndown to the 5 most relevant chunks for prompt context. The\nMistral medium-latest model then generates the final response.\nTable I shows the pipeline configurations.\nTABLE I\nPIPELINE CONFIGURATIONS\nParameter Value\nChunking method Recursive chunking\nEmbedding model mistral-embed\nVector database Qdrant\nSimilarity MedCPT Cross-Encoder\nLLM Model Mistral Medium-latest\nDataset PubMed abstracts\nIV. RESULT\nThis section presents the evaluation setup, metrics, and\nresults used to assess the effectiveness of the proposed RAG\npipeline.\nA. Evaluation Setup\nThe evaluation is conducted using medical question-answer\npairs from the PubMedQA dataset [27] with a total of 100\nquestions which are initially selected. But only 93 queries\nyield sufficient supporting documents and are included in the\nfinal evaluation. Each query should have sufficient knowledge\ncorpus which are ingested using fetched PubMed abstract.\nRetrieval is evaluated using standard metrics — Recall@K,\n\n-- 3 of 6 --\n\nFig. 2. Retrieval-Augmented Generation pipeline.\nPrecision@K, and Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR). Within\nrecall evaluation, the amount of relevant documents is de-\ntermined by similarity score between gold set and chunk\ncandidates, candidates with similarity score above 0 (non-\nnegative) will be treated as semantically relevant.\nLet:\nG = {g1, g2, . . . , gm} (2)\nC = {c1, c2, . . . , cn} (3)\ns(gi, cj ) = similarity score between gi and cj (4)\nThen for each chunk:\nSj = max\ngi∈G s(gi, cj ) (5)\nNumber of relevant chunks:\nnumRelevant = |{j | Sj > 0}| (6)\nEach chunk is considered relevant if it matches at least one\ngold answer, using the maximum similarity score across all\ngold elements. The generated responses are compared against\nreference answers using automatic metrics such as BLEU\nand ROUGE [29] [30]. Similarity scoring by Biomedical\nBERT model, Microsoft BiomedBERT [31], is also used as\na generation evaluation metric. All evaluations are conducted\nunder a fixed configuration, and results are reported as the\naverage across all valid evaluation queries.\nB. Evaluation Metrics\nThe evaluation in this research is conducted at two lev-\nels: retrieval evaluation and generation evaluation. Recall@K\nmeasures whether relevant chunks appear within the top-\nK retrieved results for a given query, while Precision@K\nevaluates the proportion of retrieved chunks in the top-K that\nare actually relevant. Mean Reciprocal Rank (MRR) assesses\nranking quality by considering the position of the first relevant\nchunk, giving higher scores when relevant results appear\nearlier in the retrieval list. Retrieval evaluation measures how\neffectively the system retrieves relevant document chunks from\nthe knowledge base for a given query. The formulas for each\nmetrics are:\nRecall@K =\nNumber of relevant documents\nretrieved in top K\nTotal number of relevant documents (7)\nPrecision@K =\nNumber of relevant documents\nretrieved in top K\nK (8)\nMRR = 1\n|Q|\n|Q|	\nX\ni=1\n1\nranki\n(9)\nWhere:\n• K: Number of top retrieved documents considered.\n\n-- 4 of 6 --\n\n• Q: Total number of queries in the evaluation dataset.\nGeneration evaluation measures the similarity between\nground-truth answers and responses generated by the language\nmodel using the retrieved context. BLEU evaluates n-gram\nprecision, while ROUGE measures lexical recall and content\ncoverage. In contrast, BERTScore evaluates semantic simi-\nlarity using contextual embeddings rather than exact lexical\noverlap.\nC. Evaluation Result\nThis section presents the retrieval and generation evaluation\nresults of the proposed multi-layer reranking RAG pipeline\non the medical question-answering dataset PubMedQA. For\nretrieval evaluation, an ablation analysis was conducted to\ncompare retrieval metrics across different reranking setups.\nThe average results for both retrieval and generation evalu-\nations are presented in Table II and Table III.\n1) Retrieval Evaluation Result: Table II presents the av-\nerage retrieval evaluation results across different reranking\nsetups. For the tag-only and topic-only ablation setups, a two-\ncomponent scheme is used: 0.80 for the base vector score\nand 0.20 for the respective signal. The full reranking setup\nTABLE II\nABLATION ANALYSIS FOR RETRIEVAL RESULTS\nQuery Count: 93\nMetric None Full Tag Only Topic Only\nRecall@K 0.9792 0.9839 0.9812 0.9789\nPrecision@K 0.3458 0.3484 0.3484 0.3474\nMRR 0.9314 0.9041 0.9108 0.9254\ndemonstrates strong retrieval effectiveness, achieving a Re-\ncall@K of 98.38% and a Precision@K of 34.84%. Compared\nto the configuration without reranking layers, which achieved\na Recall@K of 97.92%, the inclusion of topic- and tag-based\nreranking slightly improves retrieval coverage. Although the\nMRR score of the full reranking setup is slightly lower than\nthe baseline configuration, the results indicate that topic- and\ntag-based reranking can still contribute to retrieval coverage\nand hierarchical relevance prioritization within the retrieved\ncandidates. The moderate Precision@K suggests the presence\nof subtle noise among candidates. Overall, these findings sug-\ngest that the proposed multi-layer reranking approach is able to\nmaintain broad retrieval coverage while applying hierarchical\nmetadata signals into the ranking process.\n2) Generation Evaluation Result: Table III presents the\naverage generation evaluation results using the full reranking\nlayer. For generation, BLEU-4 averages at 0.2311% along\nwith ROUGE-1, ROUGE-2, and ROUGE-L scores of 39.89%,\n7.78%, and 12.41%. These indicate moderate lexical overlap\nwith the gold set, reflecting the difficulty of generating identi-\ncal medical responses. BERT score shows a high semantic sim-\nilarity between generated answer and truth answer, averaging\nat 96.99%. The generation performance is highly influenced by\nboth retrieval quality and capability of the language models.\nTABLE III\nGENERATION EVALUATION RESULTS FOR FULL RERANKING LAYER\nQuery Count: 93\nMetric Result\nBLEU-4 0.002311014\nROUGE-1 0.39895988\nROUGE-2 0.07788335\nROUGE-L 0.124113955\nBERTScore 0.9699981\n3) Analysis: The result highlights the performance of the\nproposed multi-layer reranking strategy using topic and tag\nsimilarity as additional signals to improve retrieval relevance.\nThe ablation analysis shows that applying tag- and topic-\nbased reranking enhances retrieval coverage, shown by high\nRecall@K compared to the one without reranking layers.\nHowever, a slight decrease in MRR indicates trade-offs in top-\nrank document ordering. The findings suggest that structured\nretrieval signals can improve query-document alignment and\nsupport broader retrieval relevance in medical document re-\ntrieval. A high BERT score indicates that given the refined\ndocuments as context, a general-purposed model can generate\na semantically similar answer to the truth answer.\nV. DISCUSSION\nMoreover, the proposed RAG pipeline has potential appli-\ncability as a knowledge retrieval component within Healthcare\nDigital Twin systems. By integrating semantic retrieval with\nhierarchical topic- and tag-based reranking, the framework\nmay support more context-aware biomedical information ac-\ncess in digital healthcare environments. However, the current\nevaluation is limited to curated biomedical datasets and man-\nually compiled MeSH-based queries. Further validation using\nreal-world clinical and uncurated healthcare data is required\nto assess scalability and practical applicability.\nVI. CONCLUSION\nThis research explores and evaluates a multi-layer approach\nfor RAG reranking in the medical domain, combining topic\nand tag similarity with vector retrieval. The method yields\ndecent query-document alignment and strong retrieval per-\nformance, with high Recall@K and MRR scores. Genera-\ntion quality, measured by BLEU-4 and ROUGE, remains\nmoderate, indicating the limitations of lexical similarity due\nto the variability of medical question answering. On the\nother hand, the BERTScore remains high, indicating strong\nsemantic similarity between generated and ground-truth an-\nswers. The results suggest that structured signals can con-\ntribute to retrieval effectiveness under the current settings,\nalthough with trade-offs. However, several limitations remain,\nincluding evaluation on only 93 successful queries from a\nsingle curated dataset, dependence on manually compiled\nMeSH-based queries, and uncertain generalizability to real-\nworld uncurated medical questions. Hence, further validation\nusing real-world healthcare data is necessary. Overall, this\n\n-- 5 of 6 --\n\nresearch demonstrates that integrating topic and tag signals\nwithin a hierarchical structure as layer of RAG reranking\npipeline can improve retrieval coverage and support structured\nprioritization in medical information retrieval task, although\ntrade-offs in ranking performance remain observable. The\nresults highlight the potential of metadata-enhanced retrieval\nstrategies for improving domain-specific information retrieval.\nDATA AVAILABILITY\nThe datasets used in this study are publicly available.\nThe original PubMedQA dataset can be accessed at:\nhttps://raw.githubusercontent.com/pubmedqa/pubmedqa/\nrefs/heads/master/data/ori pqal.json. A preprocessed\nversion of the dataset used in this work is available at:\nhttps://huggingface.co/datasets/llamafactory/PubMedQA. The\nimplementation code and experimental results are available\nin an anonymized repository and will be released upon\nacceptance.\nCONTRIBUTORSHIP\nAuthor contributions are omitted for double-blind review\nand will be provided upon acceptance.\nREFERENCES\n[1] H. Kilicoglu, F. Ensan, B. McInnes, and L. L. Wang, “Semantics-enabled\nbiomedical literature analytics,” Journal of Biomedical Informatics, vol.\n150, p. 104588, 2024, doi: 10.1016/j.jbi.2024.104588.\n[2] Y. Ding, W. Fan, L. Ning, S. Wang, H. Li, D. Yin, T.-S. Chua, and\nQ. Li, “A survey on RAG meets LLMs: Towards retrieval-augmented\nlarge language models,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.06211, 2024, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2405.06211.\n[3] S. Shah, S. Ryali, and R. Venkatesh, “SRAG: RAG with structured data\nimproves vector retrieval,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2603.26670, 2026, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2603.26670.\n[4] T. Pandit, S. Mahendru, M. Raval, and D. Upadhyay, “The evolution\nof reranking models in information retrieval: From heuristic methods\nto large language models,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2512.16236, 2025, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2512.16236.\n[5] E. Yu et al., “Large language models in medicine: Applications, chal-\nlenges, and future directions,” International Journal of Medical Sciences,\nvol. 22, no. 11, pp. 2792–2801, 2025, doi: 10.7150/ijms.111780.\n[6] C. Liu et al., “Domain-aware healthcare chatbot incorporating BERT\nand RAG,” in Digitalization and Management Innovation IV, A. Lau\nand A. J. Tall´on-Ballesteros, Eds. Amsterdam, Netherlands: IOS Press,\n2025, doi: 10.3233/FAIA250729.\n[7] F. Neha, D. Bhati, and D. K. Shukla, “Retrieval-Augmented Generation\n(RAG) in Healthcare: A Comprehensive Review,” AI, vol. 6, p. 226,\n2025, doi: 10.3390/ai6090226.\n[8] R. Patil, M. Abbidi, and S. Fannon, “RAGMed: A RAG-based medical\nAI assistant for improving healthcare delivery,” AI, vol. 6, no. 10, p.\n240, 2025, doi: 10.3390/ai6100240.\n[9] A. Cummings, X. Zhang, M. Olaniran, and M. Akintomide, “RAG\npipeline for domain specific applications: A case study in disseminating\ndementia care practices,” in Proc. ACM/IEEE Int. Conf. Connected\nHealth: Applications, Systems and Engineering Technologies (CHASE),\n2025, pp. 139–143, doi: 10.1145/3721201.3721396.\n[10] B. Mo¨ell and F. S. Aronsson, “Harm reduction strategies for thoughtful\nuse of large language models in the medical domain: Perspectives for\npatients and clinicians,” J. Med. Internet Res., vol. 27, 2025, Art. no.\ne75849, doi: 10.2196/75849.\n[11] Z. Al Nazi and W. Peng, “Large language models in healthcare and\nmedical domain: A review,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2401.06775, 2024, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2401.06775.\n[12] W. X. Zhao et al., “A survey of large language models,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2303.18223, 2023, doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2303.18223.\n[13] E. M. Bender, T. Gebru, A. McMillan-Major, and S. Shmitchell, “On\nthe dangers of stochastic parrots: Can language models be too big?” in\nProc. ACM Conf. Fairness, Accountability, Transparency (FAccT), 2021,\npp. 610–623.\n[14] A. T. Kalai, O. Nachum, S. S. Vempala, and E. Zhang, “Why lan-\nguage models hallucinate,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.04664, 2025, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2509.04664.\n[15] H. Soudani, E. Kanoulas, and F. Hasibi, “Fine tuning vs. retrieval\naugmented generation for less popular knowledge,” arXiv preprint\narXiv:2403.01432, 2024, doi: 10.48550/arXiv.2403.01432.\n[16] A. Gutowska, “Implement RAG chunking strategies with\nLangChain and watsonx.ai,” IBM, 2026. [Online]. Available:\nhttps://www.ibm.com/think/tutorials/chunking-strategies-for-rag-with-\nlangchain-watsonx-ai\n[17] A. Kshirsagar, “Enhancing RAG performance through chunking and text\nsplitting techniques,” Int. J. Sci. Res. Comput. Sci. Eng. Inf. Technol.,\nvol. 10, no. 5, pp. 151–158, 2024, doi: 10.32628/CSEIT2410593.\n[18] J. Zhang, W. Cui, Y. Huang, K. Das, and S. Kumar, “Synthetic\nknowledge ingestion: Towards knowledge refinement and injection for\nenhancing large language models,” in Proc. Conf. Empirical Methods\nin Natural Language Processing (EMNLP), Miami, FL, USA, 2024, pp.\n21456–21473.\n[19] Y. Gao et al., “Retrieval-augmented generation for large language\nmodels: A survey,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2312.10997, 2023, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2312.10997.\n[20] W.-W. Yim, A. B. Abacha, Z. Yu, R. Doerning, F. Xia, and M. Yetis-\ngen, “MORQA: Benchmarking evaluation metrics for medical open-\nended question answering,” arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.12405, 2025, doi:\n10.48550/arXiv.2509.12405.\n[21] T. Zhang, V. Kishore, F. Wu, K. Q. Weinberger, and Y. Artzi,\n“BERTScore: Evaluating text generation with BERT,” in Proc. Int. Conf.\nLearning Representations (ICLR), 2020.\n[22] Y. Prayitno, A. Nurfadilah, S. B. Saudi, and W. D. Tsunami, “Conver-\nsational agent for medical question-answering using RAG and LLM,”\nJournal of Artificial Intelligence and Engineering Applications (JAIEA),\nvol. 4, no. 3, pp. 1894–1899, 2025, doi: 10.59934/jaiea.v4i3.1077.\n[23] G. Luo an